Use native `<button>` elements with accessible names, visible focus indicators, and larger click targets. The icons can stay 16 × 16 pixels; the buttons should be larger.

```tsx
type IconToolbarProps = {
  onDelete: () => void;
  onShare: () => void;
};

export function IconToolbar({ onDelete, onShare }: IconToolbarProps) {
  return (
    <div className="toolbar" role="group" aria-label="Item actions">
      <button
        type="button"
        className="icon-btn"
        onClick={onDelete}
        aria-label="Delete"
      >
        <span className="icon-btn__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
          <TrashIcon />
        </span>
      </button>
      <button
        type="button"
        className="icon-btn"
        onClick={onShare}
        aria-label="Share"
      >
        <span className="icon-btn__glyph" aria-hidden="true">
          <ShareIcon />
        </span>
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Replace the old `.icon-btn:focus { outline: none; }` rule with these styles:

```css
.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  gap: 8px;
}

.icon-btn {
  display: inline-grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-width: 44px;
  min-height: 44px;
}

.icon-btn__glyph,
.icon-btn__glyph > svg {
  display: block;
  width: 16px;
  height: 16px;
}

.icon-btn:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid currentColor;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
```

Native buttons are reachable with Tab and activate with Enter or Space without custom keyboard handlers. `type="button"` prevents accidental form submission. A named group keeps ordinary Tab navigation; an ARIA `toolbar` would imply an arrow-key navigation pattern. If Delete removes the focused button, move focus to a logical next control after the deletion.

Use native buttons so both actions work with Tab, Enter, and Space. Give each button an accessible name, keep the icons decorative, and enlarge the hit targets without enlarging the icons.

